  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Showers and thunderstorms increase in coverage later\ntoday and tonight and persist into Monday. There is some potential\nfor areas of heavy rain leading to a risk of flash flooding. There\nis also a risk for strong to severe thunderstorms.\n\nAn upper-level trough will shift across eastern Canada into early \nnext week. While this occurs, strong energy crests a ridge in the \nWest and this carves the trough out more into the Tennessee and Ohio \nvalleys today and tonight. There continues to be signals in model\nguidance showing this trough potentially becoming closed off into\nearly next week as it gradually approaches the East. The main trough\nmay detach and depart to the northeast leaving behind a lingering\nclosed low. This scenario would lead to the system slowing its \neastward progression through the first half of next week. The model\ntrends continue to show a slower arrival of this system, with the\nbulk of the showers and thunderstorms occurring later tonight and\nespecially on Monday.\n\nAhead of the strengthening upper-level trough, deep south to southwest\nflow will increase and this will ramp up the low-level moisture and\ntemperature advection. It will therefore be a humid time frame as\nthe precipitable waters surge to 2+ inches. There looks to be multiple\nrounds of showers and thunderstorms, starting with an initial round\nlater tonight into Monday with the increased warm air advection regime.\nInstability will increase, however the timing at night should limit\nthe thunderstorm intensity especially since it may be more of elevated\nconvection initially. Areas of heavy rain however will be probable\ngiven the increasing forcing for ascent and high water content air.\nWhen looking at model soundings, the high precipitable water values,\nwarm cloud layer processes, and tall skinny CAPE profiles show the\nheavy rain potential. For tonight into Monday, the Weather Prediction\nCenter continues with a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) for excessive\nrainfall. \n\nOn Monday, ripples of energy along the incoming cold front during\nthe day may enhance the convective potential and with deep southerly\nflow, some training convection will be possible. Excessive rainfall\nrates would enhance the flash flooding risk, especially from the\nI-95 corridor to the north and west where the elevation rise could\nboost the ascent some given a southeast to south low-level flow.\nThere is a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 4) for Excessive rainfall\nfor Monday into Monday night for mainly northern portions of New\nJersey. There is a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) for the rest\nof our area. \n\nIn terms of the severe weather risk, the SPC has parts of eastern\nPennsylvania and northeast Maryland in a Marginal risk for severe\nthunderstorms today/tonight. On Monday, the Marginal risk moves\nto cover eastern Pennsylvania and northern/central NJ. The main\nhazard for both days will be damaging winds which will accompany\nwater loaded downbursts. \n\nBeyond Monday night, the front will weaken across the area Tuesday\nand we'll have to continue with chances (25% to 35%) for showers/tstms\non Tuesday with the very warm and humid airmass remaining in\nplace.",
